// Dependency automation for this repo. Renovate, not Dependabot: this project has
// six deliberate HOLDS whose reasoning lives in prose - in .csproj comments and
// CLAUDE.md - and Dependabot's `ignore` entries cannot express any of them. The
// point of everything below is to stop a bot cheerfully re-proposing, every week,
// the exact bump this project has already considered and rejected; and, where a
// bump IS wanted, to make the reason travel attached to the proposal.
//
// THIS FILE IS INERT UNTIL THE RENOVATE GITHUB APP IS INSTALLED. Adding it starts
// no automation by itself (see https://github.com/apps/renovate). As of this
// writing the app has never run here: no bot PRs, no bot commits, and no
// Dependency Dashboard issue despite `dependencyDashboard: true` below.
//
// The app is intended to be installed with "Automated PRs", "Require config file"
// and "Create onboarding PRs". Two consequences worth knowing:
//   - "Require config file" means Renovate does NOTHING, SILENTLY, if this file is
//     absent or unparseable ON THE DEFAULT BRANCH. That is indistinguishable from
//     "no updates available". A config that fails to parse is not a noisy error,
//     it is a bot that appears to be working and is not. Validate every edit:
//       npx --yes --package renovate -- renovate-config-validator
//   - Onboarding PRs are moot while this file exists; onboarding only fires when
//     there is no config at all.
//
// `dependencyDashboardApproval` is deliberately NOT set. It would require ticking
// a box per update before any PR appears, which re-imposes scan-only behaviour at
// the config layer and quietly defeats the app's "Automated PRs" setting. PRs are
// wanted; what is not wanted is anything MERGING unread - see `automerge` below.
//
// This file lives in .github/ rather than the repo root (it was moved there, so
// `git log --follow` reaches its history). Renovate reads either location, but
// only one may exist: a second copy at the root is a config error, not an override.
{
  $schema: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",

  extends: [
    "config:recommended",
    // Pin every GitHub Action to a commit SHA rather than a moving tag, and keep
    // the trailing `# vX.Y.Z` comment beside it updated. All four actions in this
    // repo - actions/checkout, actions/setup-node, actions/setup-dotnet and
    // cloudflare/wrangler-action - were converted from moving tags to SHAs by the
    // preset's first PR (#61). Adopting the preset makes pinned-vs-tag the rule for
    // anything added later, so the choice cannot drift action by action.
    //
    // WORTH KNOWING WHEN REVIEWING THE RE-PIN PRs THIS NOW PRODUCES: a digest update
    // carries no release timestamp, so `minimumReleaseAge` below has nothing to
    // measure and `renovate/stability-days` sits PENDING rather than ever going
    // green. Waiting does not clear it. #61 was merged with that check outstanding,
    // after verifying each SHA against the live tag ref by hand - which is the check
    // that actually matters here, since a wrong SHA is the whole risk that pinning
    // exists to remove. Expect to do the same on every re-pin.
    "helpers:pinGitHubActionDigests",
  ],

  // Let a release sit before proposing it - cheap protection against a compromised
  // or immediately-yanked publish, which matters most for the npm build tooling.
  //
  // This value is a JUDGEMENT CALL, not a constraint inherited from a package
  // manager. This repo is npm (src/vue/package-lock.json, lockfileVersion 3) plus
  // NuGet; there is no pnpm anywhere, so none of pnpm 11's `minimum-release-age`
  // cooldown behaviour - and its habit of writing a `minimumReleaseAgeExclude:`
  // bypass when asked to install something younger - is in play here. If this repo
  // ever moves to pnpm, revisit this line and the `vulnerabilityAlerts` one below
  // together, and treat any `minimumReleaseAgeExclude:` appearing in a bot PR's
  // diff as proof the PR is wrong.
  minimumReleaseAge: "3 days",

  // NOTHING MERGES UNREAD. There are no exceptions below, including GitHub Action
  // digest re-pins, and this is a decision on record rather than an omission.
  //
  // A green CI run proves this repo is CONSISTENT, not that a bump is CORRECT. The
  // counterexample on record is from a sibling project: pako 3.0.0 flipped a hash
  // default in a way that passes a green suite while silently breaking a
  // byte-exactness invariant, and the resulting wrong belief survived about a year.
  // The same shape of failure is available here - the planner's correctness lives
  // in Verify snapshots, and a dependency that changed planner output subtly would
  // show up as a snapshot diff to be accepted rather than as an obvious failure.
  //
  // IF THIS IS EVER REVISITED, automerge has two prerequisites that are easy to
  // miss and neither is satisfied today:
  //   1. Branch protection on `main` with a CI check REQUIRED, `bypass_actors`
  //      empty, and `strict_required_status_checks_policy: true`. Without all
  //      three a PR can merge red, or merge having been tested against a different
  //      `main` than the one it lands on. Note this repo's CI is three separate
  //      checks ("Build front-end", "Test .NET with Lua settings", "Test .NET with
  //      default settings"), so all three have to be required, not just one.
  //   2. Repo-level "Allow auto-merge" enabled (`allow_auto_merge` in the repo
  //      settings API). Renovate's `platformAutomerge` cannot do anything without
  //      it, and when it is off Renovate silently falls back to polling and merging
  //      the PR itself - the weaker path, because GitHub is no longer the thing
  //      gating the merge. The symptom is a PR sitting unmerged while its body
  //      cheerfully reads "Automerge: Enabled".
  automerge: false,

  // One dashboard issue is the cheapest way to see everything Renovate is holding
  // back and why - the `description` on each rule below shows up there.
  dependencyDashboard: true,
  dependencyDashboardTitle: "Dependency dashboard",

  // Without this the schedule below is UTC, so "before 6am Monday" fires around
  // midnight Sunday local and reads as a bot running at random times.
  timezone: "America/Los_Angeles",

  // One weekly batch instead of a trickle. To pull a run forward without editing
  // this file, tick the "Check this box to trigger a request for Renovate to run
  // again" checkbox on the dependency dashboard issue.
  schedule: ["before 6am on monday"],

  // Cap the burst. The first run after enabling the app sees every pending update
  // at once; without limits that is a dozen PRs in one morning, each triggering
  // three CI jobs that take ~6 minutes.
  prConcurrentLimit: 3,
  prHourlyLimit: 2,

  // DELIBERATELY ENABLED, and this is the one place this config departs from the
  // sibling project's, so the reasoning for both sides is recorded here.
  //
  // Against: `lockFileMaintenance` is an automated whole-lockfile re-resolve. In
  // the sibling pnpm repo that is the single dependency operation measured to cause
  // breakage - re-resolving ~22 surrounding transitive packages triggered a type
  // error that installing the same target versions directly did not - so it is
  // pinned off there.
  //
  // For, and why it wins here: that measurement is pnpm-specific and does not
  // transfer to this repo, which is npm and has recorded the opposite. This is the
  // mechanism that closed the fast-uri / dompurify / brace-expansion advisories
  // (PR #30), none of which had a direct-dependency bump available - they needed
  // transitive lockfile updates and nothing else would have produced them. Turning
  // it off would leave transitive-only advisories with no automated route to
  // closing.
  //
  // So: enabled, but not unattended - `automerge: false` above covers this too, and
  // a re-resolve PR is exactly the kind whose diff deserves reading. If one of
  // these ever breaks the build in a way a targeted bump would not have, that is
  // the evidence to flip it, and this comment is where to record it.
  lockFileMaintenance: {
    enabled: true,
    schedule: ["before 5am on monday"],
  },

  // Security fixes must not wait for the weekly window. Without this block a CVE
  // fix could sit ~10 days: up to 3 for the age floor, then up to 7 for Monday.
  // `schedule: []` clears the weekly restriction for these PRs only.
  //
  // The age floor drops to 25 hours rather than to zero. A same-day publish is the
  // worst case for a compromised-package attack, and an advisory is not a reason to
  // abandon the release-age guard entirely - 25 hours keeps a full day of exposure
  // to other people catching a bad publish, while cutting the wait from 3 days to
  // about 1. (25 rather than 24 is also the value that would clear pnpm 11's
  // cooldown with an hour to spare, should this repo ever switch package managers.)
  vulnerabilityAlerts: {
    schedule: [],
    minimumReleaseAge: "25 hours",
    prBodyNotes: [
      "Security update - this deliberately bypassed the weekly schedule. Still read it. Advisory version ranges are frequently flat and over-broad, so `npm audit` being red is not by itself evidence that the proposed bump is the fix.",
    ],
  },

  // GitHub's own advisories miss ecosystem-only reports; OSV catches more.
  osvVulnerabilityAlerts: true,

  // The submodules (FluteSharp, delaunator-sharp, CSharp.lua) are pinned to specific
  // commits and are upstream repos, not ours to bump. Renovate disables this manager
  // by default; stated explicitly so nobody "fixes" it later.
  "git-submodules": {
    enabled: false,
  },

  packageRules: [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Grouping
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      // Includes the SHA re-pins produced by `helpers:pinGitHubActionDigests`
      // above, which recur forever - one PR for all of them, not four.
      description: "Group all GitHub Actions bumps into one PR - they share a single CI gate.",
      matchManagers: ["github-actions"],
      groupName: "github actions",
    },
    {
      description: "Group the Vue/Vite front-end toolchain; these move together in practice.",
      matchManagers: ["npm"],
      matchPackageNames: ["vite", "vitest", "@vitejs/plugin-vue", "vue-tsc"],
      groupName: "vue build toolchain",
    },
    {
      description: "Group lint/format tooling - low risk, one review is enough.",
      matchManagers: ["npm"],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "eslint",
        "eslint-plugin-vue",
        "prettier",
        "@vue/eslint-config-prettier",
        "@vue/eslint-config-typescript",
      ],
      groupName: "lint and format tooling",
    },

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Documented holds. Each mirrors a comment in the code; keep the two in sync.
    // `allowedVersions` is used rather than `enabled: false` so patches within the
    // held line still flow - the hold is on the major, not on maintenance.
    //
    // These match by package NAME, deliberately. A rule narrowed with a
    // `matchDepTypes` guess that does not match what Renovate actually reports
    // silently matches nothing, and no validator can catch that.
    //
    // TRACKING ISSUES. Holds come in two shapes and only one of them is worth an
    // issue:
    //
    //   Migration-shaped - there is real work that would lift the hold, so the
    //   plan lives in an issue and the rule points at it:
    //     typescript ............... #49  (upstream-gated: vue-tsc vs TS 7)
    //     WebApp OpenAPI stack ..... #47  (OpenApi 10.x + Swashbuckle 10.x)
    //
    //   The Verify family used to be listed here against #44. That migration
    //   landed - the test project is on xunit v3 + Verify.XunitV3 - so the version
    //   ceiling is gone. Only the grouping remains, and it is not a hold.
    //
    //   Conditional - nothing to plan until an external circumstance changes, so
    //   there is deliberately NO issue and the revisit condition is stated in the
    //   rule itself. Filing issues for these would just create things to close:
    //     SixLabors.ImageSharp ..... needs a Six Labors license key
    //     @types/node .............. tracks CI's Node, not the registry
    //     ApplicationInsights ...... only matters if WebApp is hosted again
    //
    // The live inventory of everything held back is dependency dashboard #41,
    // which is a better place to look than reading manifests by hand - it is how
    // the missing swashbuckle.aspnetcore.cli hold was found (#43).
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      // Tracking issue: #49. Upstream-gated - nothing here unblocks it. Expected to
      // be a small bump whenever it does unblock, which is exactly why it is worth
      // tracking rather than re-deriving the refusal each time the bot proposes it.
      description:
        "HOLD typescript on 6.x. TypeScript 7 is the Go rewrite and does not ship the programmatic compiler API that vue-tsc/Volar embeds, so Vue template type-checking breaks. Note vue-tsc's peer range is an open '>=5.0.0' and would happily install TS 7 - the peer range is not permission. Unlock when TS 7.1 ships the API and vue-tsc announces support - tracked in #49.",
      matchManagers: ["npm"],
      matchPackageNames: ["typescript"],
      allowedVersions: "<7.0.0",
    },
    {
      // No tracking issue by design - this is not a deferred upgrade. It tracks
      // whatever Node CI runs on, so it moves when that moves and never on its own.
      description:
        "HOLD @types/node on 24.x to match the Node 24 used by CI and the Cloudflare deploy. It should track the runtime, not the registry. Raise this and the workflows' node-version together.",
      matchManagers: ["npm"],
      matchPackageNames: ["@types/node"],
      allowedVersions: "<25.0.0",
      prBodyNotes: [
        "`node-version: 24` is hardcoded in both .github/workflows/ci.yml and .github/workflows/deploy-cloudflare.yml - there is no .node-version or .nvmrc file for Renovate to bump instead. If this ceiling is ever raised, those two workflows must move in the same PR, or the types will describe a runtime CI is not using.",
      ],
    },
    {
      // No tracking issue by design - there is nothing to plan. The hold lifts if
      // and only if someone registers a Six Labors license key; until then there is
      // no migration to schedule, just a condition to state. Applies to the
      // .Drawing rule below too, which must move with this one.
      description:
        "HOLD SixLabors.ImageSharp on 3.x. ImageSharp 4.x adds build-time license enforcement and fails the build without a Six Labors license key. See the comment in src/FactorioTools/FactorioTools.csproj. Debug-visualizer-only, never ships. Unlock only if someone registers a key (free for OSS qualifies).",
      matchManagers: ["nuget"],
      matchPackageNames: ["SixLabors.ImageSharp"],
      allowedVersions: "<4.0.0",
    },
    {
      description:
        "HOLD SixLabors.ImageSharp.Drawing on 2.x - same license enforcement as ImageSharp 4.x. Must move together with ImageSharp.",
      matchManagers: ["nuget"],
      matchPackageNames: ["SixLabors.ImageSharp.Drawing"],
      allowedVersions: "<3.0.0",
    },
    {
      // Tracking issue: #47 - the OpenApi 10.x + Swashbuckle 10.x migration that
      // lifts this whole group. Read it before touching any ceiling here; the two
      // halves have different ones and the reasons are not interchangeable.
      //
      // THE CLI IS PART OF THIS STACK AND WAS MISSING FROM THE FIRST VERSION OF
      // THIS RULE. `swashbuckle.aspnetcore.cli` lives in
      // src/WebApp/.config/dotnet-tools.json (currently 6.9.0), not in any .csproj,
      // so reading the project files alone does not reveal it - and because it is a
      // separate package name, matching "Swashbuckle.AspNetCore" never covered it.
      // Renovate promptly queued a jump straight to v10 for it. That CLI is what
      // WebApp.csproj's PostBuild target runs to generate the committed
      // swagger.json, which in turn generates the Vue client, so a v10 generator
      // against a 6.x library is exactly the piecemeal move the csproj comment
      // forbids. It is held here with the rest of the stack.
      //
      // CEILING IS <7.0.0, not the <9.0.0 originally guessed. Installed today are
      // Swashbuckle.AspNetCore 6.9.0 and swashbuckle.aspnetcore.cli 6.9.0, and
      // nothing in the csproj comment establishes that 7.x or 8.x is safe against
      // Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 8.0.x - it reasons only about 6.x (current)
      // and 10.x (the compatible target). <9.0.0 would have waved through a
      // Swashbuckle v8 major on that unexamined basis. Patches within 6.x still
      // flow; the hold is on the major.
      description:
        "HOLD the WebApp OpenAPI stack at its current majors, and keep all of it in one PR - these move together or not at all. Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 9.0+ pulls Microsoft.OpenApi 2.x, whose schema model Swashbuckle 6.x cannot consume; Swashbuckle 10.x adopts 2.x as well, so OpenApi 10.x + Swashbuckle 10.x + CLI 10.x is the compatible set. Getting there is a real migration (rewrite of OilFieldPlanRequestDefaultsSchemaFilter.cs, edits to RequireNonNullablePropertiesSchemaFilter.cs, regenerated swagger.json and Vue client), deferred because WebApp is not deployed. See the comment in src/WebApp/WebApp.csproj.",
      matchManagers: ["nuget"],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi",
        "Swashbuckle.AspNetCore",
        "swashbuckle.aspnetcore.cli",
      ],
      groupName: "WebApp OpenAPI stack",
      allowedVersions: "<9.0.0",
    },
    {
      // Split out because the two halves of the stack have different ceilings:
      // Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi is held below 9.0.0 (that is where the
      // Microsoft.OpenApi 2.x break lands), while the Swashbuckle packages are held
      // below 7.0.0 for the reason above. `allowedVersions` is last-match-wins, so
      // this narrows the two Swashbuckle entries without disturbing the grouping or
      // the OpenApi ceiling set by the rule above.
      description:
        "HOLD both Swashbuckle packages on 6.x specifically. Installed: Swashbuckle.AspNetCore 6.9.0, swashbuckle.aspnetcore.cli 6.9.0 - the two were out of step until #63 brought the CLI up to match, which is what this rule existing at all is meant to keep true. The csproj rationale covers 6.x and 10.x only - 7.x/8.x are simply unexamined against Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 8.0.x, so they are not a safe default. Raise this only as part of the OpenApi 10.x migration, which moves the whole stack at once - tracked in #47.",
      matchManagers: ["nuget"],
      matchPackageNames: ["Swashbuckle.AspNetCore", "swashbuckle.aspnetcore.cli"],
      groupName: "WebApp OpenAPI stack",
      allowedVersions: "<7.0.0",
    },
    {
      // The version ceiling that used to sit here came off with #44: the test
      // project moved to xunit v3 + Verify.XunitV3, so Verify.DiffPlex is no longer
      // pinned to 3.1.x and the family tracks current again.
      //
      // The grouping stays, because the coupling that made the old hold necessary
      // is structural, not version-specific. Verify.DiffPlex and Verify.XunitV3
      // both bind to Verify core, so bumping one alone can pull core past what the
      // other supports. That mismatch is the dangerous kind - it compiles with zero
      // warnings and dies at runtime. On the old stack it took out 2748 of 4261
      // tests with MethodAccessException on Guards.AgainstBadSourceFile.
      description:
        "Keep the whole Verify family in one PR. Verify.DiffPlex and Verify.XunitV3 both bind to the Verify core package, so bumping one alone can pull core past what the other supports - which builds cleanly and then fails at runtime with MethodAccessException on Guards.AgainstBadSourceFile. Not a version hold; these are free to move, just not separately. See the comment in test/FactorioTools.Test/FactorioTools.Test.csproj.",
      matchManagers: ["nuget"],
      matchPackageNames: ["Verify.XunitV3", "Verify.DiffPlex", "Verify"],
      groupName: "Verify test snapshot stack",
      prBodyNotes: [
        "This family's failure mode BUILDS CLEANLY and dies at runtime, so a compiling branch is not evidence. Run the full `dotnet test` before believing any bump here.",
      ],
    },
    {
      // No tracking issue by design - this is a "not worth the review" call rather
      // than a deferred upgrade, and it reverses on its own if WebApp is hosted
      // again. The Dockerfile rule below is the same call for the same reason;
      // keep the two consistent.
      description:
        "SKIP major bumps of Microsoft.ApplicationInsights.AspNetCore. WebApp is no longer deployed, so telemetry majors are not worth the review. Patches still flow. Re-enable if the API is ever hosted again.",
      matchManagers: ["nuget"],
      matchPackageNames: ["Microsoft.ApplicationInsights.AspNetCore"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      // Resolves #45. Same reasoning as the ApplicationInsights rule above, applied
      // to the only Dockerfile in the repo (src/WebApp/Dockerfile - confirmed from
      // the Renovate job log: "Matched 1 file(s) for manager dockerfile"). Not
      // narrowed with matchFileNames, because there is exactly one such file and a
      // path guess that stops matching after a move would fail silently.
      //
      // Majors off, patches ON, which is the deliberate half of this. A .NET major
      // on an undeployed service is not worth a review, but base images accumulate
      // OS-level CVEs, and `10.0` / `10.0-alpine` are FLOATING minor tags - the
      // digest behind them moves on its own regardless of what Renovate proposes.
      // Keeping patches live is what lets a rebuild pick those up.
      //
      // WORTH KNOWING: global.json now pins the SDK to 10.0.302 while this
      // Dockerfile tracks whatever `10.0` currently resolves to. Those two can
      // drift apart silently, and Renovate will not report it because a floating
      // tag is never "out of date". If the container build ever behaves differently
      // from CI, check that first.
      description:
        "SKIP major bumps of the src/WebApp/Dockerfile base images (mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk, runtime-deps). WebApp is no longer deployed, so a .NET major here is not worth the review - same call as Microsoft.ApplicationInsights.AspNetCore above. Patch/minor updates still flow, because base images carry OS-level security fixes even when the service is unhosted. Re-enable if the API is ever hosted again.",
      matchManagers: ["dockerfile"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      enabled: false,
    },
  ],
}
